On Nov 30, 5:28 am, Jonadab the Unsightly One
<jonadab.theunsightly...@gmail.com> wrote:
> From upthread, it appears I may be carrying
> too many potions and scrolls.

I agree.  I tend to leave potions and scrolls in the first large box I
find, after dragging the box close to the downstair of the level I
find it (or altar, if there is one).

> > A pet in mine town should be weak enough to
> > not attack shop keepers or the priest.
>
> That has not been my experience.

Pets gain levels by gaining maxHP every time they kill something.
Dogs and cats will max out at 72HP, which makes them level 9.  Pets
will not attack a monster more than 1 level higher than it, and
shopkeepers and aligned priests will always be generated at least
level 11.  The only way your pet large dog / large cat should ever
attack those is:
a) if you're causing conflict (usually by wearing a ring), or
b) if your pet dog somehow ate a wraith corpse while already at level
9, causing it to gain a level.
You can verify this for yourself with a stethoscope.

That's why a cat/dog makes a great credit cloning servant.

> Perhaps I let my dogs kill too many monsters.  My
> first really successful game I was a tourist and
> couldn't handle melee and my pet did almost all
> of the killing in the early game, so perhaps I
> developed a bad habit there.

I disagree: this is a good habit.  A strong pet means you have more
power to kill things without having the increase in monster difficulty
you get when you level up yourself.
You do miss out on the maxHP and skill slots that you get by levelling
up though, so it's a balancing act.

> > > Also, a large dog can certainly kill a shopkeeper.  I've seen
> > > that happen in more than one game already.
>
> > I've had a pet horse attack the mine town priest even.
>
> I've only tried to keep herbivorous pets a couple of times,
> and they starved.  I usually keep one or more dogs.

I've had the same problem with vegetarian pets. Monks, knights and
some tourists are the exception.
Warhorses max out at 80HP - level 10 - and will therefore attack
shopkeepers and priests that are generated at level 11.

> I don't know about horses, but I'm pretty sure a
> maxed-out large dog can successfully kill an
> aligned priest.

It should be possible, if you have sufficient resources to heal your
pet faster than the priest heals itself, but it's difficult and you
need conflict.
I've certainly had my warhorse kill the minetown priest before, with
my help.

> On the other hand, dogs do sometimes bite
> off more than they can chew, particularly when
> the monsters are going in groups.  I try to keep
> at least one tripe ration handy in case I need
> to tame a new pet.

I find this is particularly the case with the minetown watch captain.
He's level 10, and tends to have a high damage weapon that the pet
doesn't take into account.
A wand of sleep is incredibly useful in this situation, so long as you
actually hit the captain with it - you don't want him angry and
awake.  If you can, line him up against a wall so he's got a chance of
being hit on the rebound.
Spare tripe is really worth carrying for this reason, yes.  You can
also throw it at hostile horses to make them peaceful (not tame), and
it won't get used up that way.

Have fun!
